us,United States,12063784498,2024-12-14T12:55:24-07:00,🗯 : Coming from a mobile + Desktop user,"About mobile editing: Talking from a mobile user, I use discord to chat with friends and make servers for fun, but on some privileges I have on the app are limited when it comes to server settings. On desktop you can start a community and have a lot of options, edit roles with photos, and add channels with the + button which makes it easier to make channels faster, but once you get onto mobile you don't even have those privileges. 

A thought that could be put into consideration: Most cases cause of overloading the screen with too many buttons and overwhelming. But here's an idea that can be put into consideration, there could be a developer button that allows you to edit the server with desktop privileges too so it wouldn't have to be a back and forth thing and have trouble on mobile when you're editing the server. This could be added at the top of the channels as s question and it could help some people who use the mobile version of discord and those who Don't own desktop! 

In conclusion, this could be an improvement to the Discord app for people who use mobile and have trouble editing their server without the desktop features available on mobile. Thank you for hearing me write a review and hope your team take this into consideration as an add on to the app.

- RIN ★",4,258.0,⊹ RIN,0
us,United States,11120329870,2024-04-04T04:55:05-07:00,Quit breaking the mobile app,"Every update since the unnecessary UI change in the fall has made using this app worse. Things that used to be one, maybe two clicks away are now buried in random menus with seemingly no reason as to why they were placed there. This latest update has made in app notifications take up the entire screen at times instead of just leaving the simple notification bar at the top, meaning if I'm scrolling in one server and a friend messages me in another, or god forbid multiple people start talking, my only option is to turn off in app notifications so as to be able to do anything. This is just the latest in a long list of minor but increasingly annoying changes that have been occurring since the new UI, not to mention the numerous bugs that each update brings as well. It still works ok compared to other messaging apps, but feels like there's a directive from somewhere to make changes for the sake of change, and most have not been needed.",2,223.0,J98766543,2
us,United States,3175963843,2018-09-10T14:48:35-07:00,"A great desktop & browser app, born for touch.","I’ve cut my cord with TeamSpeak LONG AGO because that’s not how one should host real-time voice chat.  All my friends were transitioning from Skype to Discord when they first heard about it, so I did too.  I cannot say I’ve regretted that choice.

Discord is basically one of those apps/services that might seem boring or difficult from a screenshot, but it is such a simple and helpful tool that I use DAILY.  How is it this good?  It combines some of the most powerful features in regular text and voice communication into a free and easy-to-use app that you can view in a web browser or download on your computer/phone.  The experience doesn’t change, regardless.

The app itself is a slimmed-down version of the original desktop version, which the servers & channels and playerlists being in their own hidden panes.  This doesn’t hold me back at all. The app runs fast and is actively being improved all the time, with new TestFlight builds releasing every few days.

I can only think of one downside that doesn’t really affect my overall love for this program: when will there be a console version of the app we can download?

To conclude, it’s the best thing since online multiplayer.  It beats having to call a ton of people just to talk to a few.  And most of all, it beats having to pay for server hosting!",5,2.3.7,simsnet1,0
